Section 1: Foundational Mechanics & Vector Dynamics
1.1 Flow Velocity vs. Static Coordinates
Standard institutional models treat space-time as a rigid, static metric grid ($g_{\mu\nu}$) in which matter resides. Under Fluid Space-Time Mechanics, space and time are re-indexed as an active, directional flow velocity vector field ($\mathbf{v}_{\text{flow}}$). Space-time is not a passive stage; it is the active fluid medium itself.
1.2 The Observer as an Impedance Mismatch (Boundary Condition)
An "observer" is defined non-anthropocentrically as any system, interaction, or localized impedance mismatch ($\Delta Z$) capable of reflecting the incoming flow wave field.

The Uncollapsed State: Represented as an open, continuous wave propagating through an unhindered medium—pure, boundless fluid potential.
The Reflection Event: When an incident flow wave ($\psi_{\text{inc}}$) encounters a localized impedance boundary ($Z_{\text{obs}}$) distinct from the background medium ($Z_0$), a reflected wave ($\psi_{\text{ref}}$) is generated.
1.3 Standing Waves and Mass Generation
The interference between the incident and reflected waves produces localized standing-wave nodes:

$$R = \frac{Z_{\text{obs}} - Z_0}{Z_{\text{obs}} + Z_0}$$

Inertial mass ($m_{\text{node}}$) is the localized energy density trapped within these resonant standing-wave interference knots:

$$m_{\text{node}} = \frac{\hbar \cdot \omega_{\text{standing}} \cdot |R|^2}{c_{\text{flow}}^2}$$
